ve  algorithm like that you do have access  to that in haskell like you're not going  to be blocked you're not going to be  like oh i guess we got to rewrite in c  plus plus no you actually can do it and  it can be competitive in performance  with languages that are 100 imperative  that are imperative all the time not  just when you're sort of doing this  carve out um  the deltas that you see here like the  reason that some of these languages are  different is basically though although  there is the same algorithm you know  behind the scenes they're all doing  asymptotically the same thing there's  different language overheads that are  sort of accounting for these differences  um  worth noting that in this benchmark we  found that tweaks affect the overhead a  lot so i really don't want anyone to  look at that and be like oh java is  faster than javascript no this is not a  reasonable conclusion to take away  for example originally we had when we  did this we used java arraylist and  someone pointed out hey java array is  like much faster it's something like  this sure enough that was true that  really brought brought things down for  java we didn't consider that to be  really a performance optimization  because it's like either was a  reasonable choice  javascript originally we were sorting  64-bit integers and even though java has  like a javascript has a big num thing  now it turns out it's just still way  slower than double which is sort of  java's javascript's red and butter so go  figure we switched everything to double  so so is not to disadvantage javascript  and c plus is skipping bounce checks  maybe that's unfair to the other  language that's definitely memory unsafe  but that is kind of idiomatic c plus  plus so we figured well we're trying to  just do a straightforward implementation  here so that seemed fair again can't  emphasize this enough this is not a  rigorous comparison  this is really just to talk about you  know performance optimization and u