on you  know unsafe things um  so one example of a good optimization  that's really common and important in  functional languages is tail call  optimization this is something where we  don't need to drop down into other  languages we don't need to do unsafe  things we can just let the compiler sort  of translate pure functional code into  code that is equivalent to the  imperative counterpart so uh basically  any imperative loop like a while or a  four or anything like that  can be expressed this is there's a proof  somewhere i forget what it's called um  as a recursive function instead they're  both they're both like equivalent in  terms of expressive power um the  recursive function by default it has  very different runtime characteristics  but tail call optimization can detect  when it's possible to optimize that into  just the same thing in other words like  the exact machine instructions that you  get out the other side are going to be  identical whether you wrote a while loop  or you wrote a recursive function  so that's cool and that's sort of  promising  could we do the same thing here could we  make it so that we could take this  imperative for loop where we did  mutation in place for this array example  and make it so that the array.map  version optimizes to exactly the same  thing like same runtime characteristics  and the answer is yes but  you've got to be careful with that  because if we're doing things in place  it's not really immutable anymore  except it can be in one situation  like if those compile to the same  machine code and  we have to happen to be in the situation  where the thing that we're mapping over  is never referenced again for the rest  of the program  there's no downside it's never  referenced again yeah we're mutating in  place but no one ever sees that  like we're just reusing the same memory  and so it's still semantically immutable  we've still preserved all of our  guarantees everything's still  referentially transparent  but we're getting the runtime  performance of imperative in place  mutation  interesting interesting so this is the  idea of opportunistic in-place mutation  basically the compiler figuring out when  it's unobservable to change to in-place  mutation and then just doing that  similar to tail call optimization um so  this absolutely can be done  automatically one way to do it is by  using reference counting like we are  using in rock so basically the trick is  just reference count counts how many  times the thing is referenced if you're  about to do something that would  ordinarily be in  immutable clone and you realize wait a  minute the reference count is one that  means nobody else is referencing it so  let's go ahead and do it in place you  can totally do that um this does mean  that you need to be doing reference  counting instead of a tracing garbage  collector because the tracing garbage  collector doesn't have that information  it doesn't know how many uh references  you have to something so um that this  optimization when done in this way sort  of relies on that  as it turns out um very very often like  a whole lot of the time though um you  can actually do this statically like you  can detect  that the reference is going to be won at  this exact point because you can see  it's just never referenced for the rest  of the program  so this is basically a way to detect  many but not quite all but many very  many of the these in-place mutation  optimization opportunities at compile  time now this means that you don't  actually need to do the runtime  reference count checks which is good  because it works with all of the  different ways that rock can manage  memory not just reference counting  and it also means that we can do it for  unboxed values so only box values are  reference counted and which means that  you know if we're doing this the static  detection we can do it for any kind of  record for example  um  this also reduces runtime overhead in  general because it